# Base: the functions written in Alchem. A string gives up its bytes by
# comparison. octets takes it one character at a time. A character of one byte
# is looked up in a map, or in a short text searched for among the 256 strings
# of one byte, which costs no map. A longer character has its first byte
# searched for and is split at that byte, and so on for the pieces; its bytes
# are written as numbers before commas and split again. Results are filled by
# Enum.map over a list of places, so that no list is built by appending and
# memory stays in proportion to the text. The forms that take options are at
# the end of the file.
# The entries without a public function (octets, to64 and so on) are shared by
# the others through needs. The string of a byte comes from four entries of 64
# bytes each, because the seed backend cannot build a compiler that holds one
# entry of all 256.
